# PART 2 — Concrete Gate Design in Lean 4.34.1 Core (No Mathlib)

## Architecture & Shared Population (Zero Second Enumeration)

* **Where it lives:** `scripts/proofs/vacuity.sh` + `scripts/proofs/vacuity.lean` (plus negative-control runner `scripts/proofs/vacuity_controls.sh`), invoked from [`scripts/checks/run.sh`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/scripts/checks/run.sh#L17-L23) alongside `axioms.sh`, `trust_ban.sh`, and `rules.sh`.
* **Why `scripts/proofs/`:** Like [`axioms.sh`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/scripts/proofs/axioms.sh), it inspects kernel `ConstantInfo` across `ConcreteSpec` (which imports both `ConcreteSpec.Spec` and `ConcreteSpec.Proofs`; see [`ConcreteSpec.lean`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/ConcreteSpec.lean#L1-L2)).
* **How it shares one population of theorems and examples with `axioms.sh` and `rules.sh`:**
  * **Finding 8 (Current population split between `theorems.lean` and `rules.sh`):** Currently, [`scripts/proofs/theorems.lean`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/scripts/proofs/theorems.lean#L16-L31) enumerates all `ConstantInfo.thmInfo` in `ConcreteSpec` modules (3,255 constants in [`axioms.golden`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/scripts/proofs/axioms.golden), including ~2,780 compiler-generated `.inj`, `.injEq`, `.eq_1`, `._proof_1`, `._simp_1`, `.sizeOf_spec` theorems), whereas [`scripts/spec/rules.sh`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/scripts/spec/rules.sh#L33-L45) copies `ConcreteSpec` to a temp workspace, runs `python3 scripts/proofs/lean_lex.py examples` to turn every `example` into `noncomputable def «⟪example⟫FILE:LINE»`, and builds `ConcreteSpec.Spec`.
  * **Unified single-population design:** Factor a shared helper into [`scripts/proofs/lib.sh`](file:///home/unbalancedparen/projects/gates-briefs/n2/scripts/proofs/lib.sh) (or a shared Lean library `scripts/proofs/Population.lean` imported by `theorems.lean`, `rules.lean`, and `vacuity.lean`):
    1. **Written user theorems vs. Lean-generated theorems:** In Lean 4.34.1 core (`import Lean`), a `ConstantInfo.thmInfo` in a `ConcreteSpec.*` module is a **user-written theorem** iff:
       - `!name.isInternal && !name.isInternalDetail`
       - `(← findDeclarationRanges? name).isSome` (Lean attaches `DeclarationRanges` only to syntax-declared constants, or we filter out structural suffixes `.inj`, `.injEq`, `.noConfusion`, `.noConfusionType`, `.rec`, `.recOn`, `.casesOn`, `.brecOn`, `.below`, `.ibelow`, `.sizeOf_spec`, `.eq_def`, `.eq_unfold`, `.eq_1`..`.eq_N`, `.elim`, `.ctorIdx`).
       - Cross-check: `Population.lean` compares the count of user-written `thmInfo` constants per module against `lean_lex.py`'s lexical `theorem` count (currently **475** on `e115d8bef`, **509** with C10). If the kernel user-theorem set and lexical `theorem` set ever diverge by even 1 name, the gate fails immediately (`POPULATION_MISMATCH`).
    2. **Single `lean_lex.py examples` build for both `rules.sh` and `vacuity.sh`:** `lean_lex.py examples` rewrites every `example` in `ConcreteSpec/{Spec,Proofs}/**/*.lean` into `noncomputable def «⟪example⟫FILE:LINE»` while preserving its preceding `/-- ... -/` doc comment. Building `ConcreteSpec` once in the `with_lean_Copy` workspace (which takes ~3.5s incremental when `.olean`s are copied from the root `lake build`) gives an environment containing **every** `Spec` and `Proofs` `theorem`, `def`, `inductive`/`structure`, AND every `«⟪example⟫FILE:LINE»` with its full `findDocString?`, `ConstantInfo.type`, and `ConstantInfo.value!`. Both `rules.lean` and `vacuity.lean` run against this single built environment!

